            <ArticleTitle>Spinal noradrenergic neurotransmission and the analgesia induced by brief footshock.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Antinociception induced by brief footshock as well as by 5-methoxy-N,N-dimethyltryptamine was antagonized by lesions of the descending bulbospinal noradrenergic (NA) pathways by intrathecal injections of 6-hydroxydopamine. The alpha 2-adrenoceptor antagonist, yohimbine, injected intrathecally also blocked both types of nociceptive effects in the tail-flick and hot-plate tests. 5-Methoxy-N,N-dimethyltryptamine (1 mg/kg) potentiated shock-induced antinociception and this potentiation was also antagonized by decreased NA neurotransmission. These findings suggest an important role for spinal NA innervation, and possibly alpha 2-adrenoceptors in antinociception induced by brief footshock and serotonergic receptor stimulation induced analgesia in rats.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
